** ( ) css

     we need a mechanism for writing the dynamic css to disk.
     it's annoying to have to wait for the colors to load and also just unnecessary.
   there's 7000 lines of css. this has to be optimized somehow. im sure theres a ton of redundant classes.
   Cell.Classes should be a set/map, not a string, maybe even create a new struct for rendering
   that way we can keep Cell as it is and even make Char of type rune

   we need a map\<int,\[\]string\> that maps from hl id to classes
   if multiple hl ids map to the same classes then we should only keep one around
   that means we need another map \<int,int\> that maps from hl id to effective hl id
   that way we can actually just compare effective hl id on cells (with one char) for tokenization
   and we dont need to duplicate the longer strings on every consecutive cell with the same hl id

   actually probably go full atomic classes
   that means we just need to create classes for every bg and fg color
   for bold,italic,underline etc we can just use tailwind classes (maybe need to make sure they are not purged)

   how do we determine the atomic classes for a given hl id?
   - for bold/italic etc. it's easy
   - for colors we need to create them dynamically

   keep two files that are loaded on startup
   one file contains a map from effective hl id to classes
   the other file contains a map from color to class
   hlAttrDefine has to wait for these files to be loaded before adding new css -> keep stuff in a "queue" until then
   hlAttrDefine should always check if this highlight is already covered by existing classes
   that mean we need a reverse map\<color,classname\> 
   check if the map has an entry for the fg/bg color
   if not create one, otherwise use existing class
   just prefix key of that map with fg/bg so we dont need two maps
   new classes can simply be named with a short unique id that is just incremented
   hl attr define needs to look up the effective hl id first 

   hl definition/maintenance:
   ~ on startup load maps from files 
   ~ determine colors class id counter with number of entries
   ~ hlAttrDefine: map from hl id to effective hl id
   ~ check if theres an entry for this hl id to get classes
   ~~ if yes -> return ; we have either already dynamically added this or it's already in the css file
   ~~ if no
   ~~~ determine classes for font stuff etc.
   ~~~ look up color classes 
   ~~~~ if one already exists -> great we can use that
   ~~~~ if not then increment the id counter and write new entry to colors file
   ~~~ add entry in id classes file
   ~ add css dynamically with existing process
   ~ in a cron job type thing write new css to a css file that is loaded on startup

   Should check how big that css file gets. Potentially there's value in splitting it up by filetype or something

   Rendering:
   ~ gridLine: map and assign effective hl id
   ~ optimizeGrid: tokenize based on effective hl id
   ~ map effective hl id to classes and assign them to the token

